{{- /*
Recipes for agents not yet on the roster: invite from an admin device first,
then join on the agent's machine, then the kind's setup.<kind> steps from
agent.tmpl, then recipe.tail. One step per line. Onboarding never mints codes;
it only tells the reader to run tincan invite.
*/ -}}

{{- define "recipe.invite" -}}
On an admin device (or on the relay host with --socket <state-dir>/admin.sock): tincan invite <name> --kind {{.Kind}}. The code is valid 10 minutes; hand it straight to the agent's machine, never through group chat or logs.
On the agent's machine: {{template "join.default" .}}
{{- end -}}
{{- define "recipe.invite.proxy-sandbox" -}}
On an admin device (or on the relay host with --socket <state-dir>/admin.sock): tincan invite <name> --kind {{.Kind}}. The code is valid 10 minutes; hand it straight to the agent's machine, never through group chat or logs.
On the agent's machine: {{template "join.proxy-sandbox" .}}
{{- end -}}
{{- define "recipe.invite.chatgpt" -}}
No tincan invite: ChatGPT cannot join the tailnet, so it connects through the relay's OAuth gateway instead.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "recipe.tail" -}}
Once <name> has joined, re-run tincan onboard: its agent block holds the standing instructions to paste, and the operator prompt then lists it.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "recipe.second-agent" -}}
Second agent on this machine
Each agent on one machine is its own invite and its own client config; the machine's Tailscale identity is shared. Example: codex beside claude-code, or openclaw beside hermes.
On an admin device: tincan invite <second-name> --kind <kind>.
On the machine: TINCAN_CONFIG=~/.config/tincan/<second-name>.json tincan join <code> --relay {{.RelayURL}}
Set TINCAN_CONFIG=~/.config/tincan/<second-name>.json in that agent's MCP server entry (its env) and in its tincan listen command, so every call names the right agent.
Leave the first agent on the default config; only the added agent needs TINCAN_CONFIG.
Check with TINCAN_CONFIG=~/.config/tincan/<second-name>.json tincan agents, then re-run tincan onboard.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "recipe.relay-host" -}}
Host the relay
Pick one always-on machine on your tailnet: a VPS, Mac mini, Raspberry Pi, home server, or an always-on agent's VM. Every agent dials out to it; nothing is public.
Run it as its own OS user so the relay database and wake.json stay out of any agent's reach. Keep it running under systemd, launchd or similar.
Host tailscaled mode (the machine already runs Tailscale): tincan relay --listen <tailscale-ip> --port 8787 --admin <admin-machines>. Agents use --relay http://<tailscale-ip>:8787.
tsnet mode (the relay joins the tailnet as its own node): TS_AUTHKEY=<auth key> tincan relay --hostname tincan-relay --admin <admin-machines> (or omit TS_AUTHKEY and follow the login URL it prints). Agents use --relay http://tincan-relay (port 80 unless --port is set).
--admin lists the untagged machines allowed to invite and remove (your laptop, your phone); add --admin-login to also require your Tailscale login. Tag agent machines (for example tag:agent) so they can never be admins. Untagged agent machines that are rebuilt with the same machine name are re-admitted automatically (tagged ones need a new invite); start the relay with --no-auto-rebind to turn that off.
State lives in --state-dir (default: the user config dir, tincan-relay). Put wake.json there with chmod 600; its secrets are never sent to agents.
On the relay host itself, admin commands use the local socket: tincan invite <name> --kind <kind> --socket <state-dir>/admin.sock.
Then add agents with the agent recipes and run tincan onboard --relay <relay-url>.
{{- end -}}
